text:
zara: FYI QA hit a reproducible failure in the Java + Kotlin sample apps: after a token refresh under flaky mobile network, a bunch of streaming calls end up 504 or cancelled. Repro on Pixel 7 and emulator Android 15.

mike: interesting. is the sample doing a blocking refresh from an interceptor? that's the usual suspect.

zara: yes. TokenInterceptor does a synchronized refreshBlocking() using the same Retrofit client. snippet: 
```kotlin
// simplified
override fun intercept(chain: Interceptor.Chain): Response {
  var tok = tokenStore.get()
  if (tok == null) {
    synchronized(lock) { if (tokenStore.get() == null) tokenStore.set(refreshBlocking())
      tok = tokenStore.get()
    }
  }
  val req = chain.request().newBuilder().header("Authorization","Bearer $tok").build()
  val res = chain.proceed(req)
  if (res.code == 401) {
    synchronized(lock) { tokenStore.set(refreshBlocking()) }
    return chain.proceed(req.newBuilder().header("Authorization","Bearer ${tokenStore.get()}").build())
  }
  return res
}
```

oliver: that pattern will consume Dispatcher threads when refreshBlocking() issues HTTP calls via the same client. Under Android 15 HTTP/2 prioritization/connection reuse changes, pooled streams can block and you see canceled streams -> 504s instead of slow responses.

jade: other issues we observed: thundering herd on token endpoint (many requests call refresh concurrently) causing 429s and short-term blacklisting.

mike: two low-friction fixes we recommend in the sample app and SDK docs: (1) use a dedicated NoAuth refresh client that has no auth interceptor and a tiny dispatcher (maxRequests=2, maxRequestsPerHost=1), (2) implement an in-flight dedupe (singleflight) so only one refresh executes and others wait for the result.

zara: implemented a Kotlin Deferred singleflight and a Java CompletableFuture AtomicReference pattern in the sample branch. Example Java gist: 
```java
private final AtomicReference<CompletableFuture<Token>> inflight = new AtomicReference<>();
public Token refreshSingleflight() {
  for (;;) {
    CompletableFuture<Token> cur = inflight.get();
    if (cur != null) return cur.join();
    CompletableFuture<Token> next = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> refreshBlockingWithRefreshClient());
    if (inflight.compareAndSet(null, next)) {
      try { return next.join(); } finally { inflight.compareAndSet(next, null); }
    }
  }
}
```

mike: also consider switching long-lived streaming calls to retryOnConnectionFailure=false so they don't hold retry loops that keep connections busy. tune callTimeout/readTimeout for mobile use.

oliver: for coroutine users we added a Mutex + Deferred pattern and a tiny refreshClient created with: 
```kotlin
val refreshClient = OkHttpClient.Builder()
  .dispatcher(Dispatcher().apply { maxRequests = 2; maxRequestsPerHost = 1 })
  .connectionPool(ConnectionPool(1, 30, TimeUnit.SECONDS))
  .build()
```

jade: QA reports that with refreshClient + singleflight the repro disappears on Android 15. We added an integration test that fires 20 parallel requests, kills the token, toggles airplane mode, and asserts no 429s from token endpoint. Test added behind a flaky-device tag.

zara: action items: update sample app to use the refresh client + singleflight, add README callout "Do not call primary OkHttp client from inside interceptors/authenticators; use a dedicated client for token endpoints," and add migration snippet for Java/Kotlin parity.

mike: small doc note: mention Android 15 http/2 prioritization change as the reason this surfaced more often; many teams will be fine on older OS.

